## Architecture

This app is built on the [platform-infra](https://github.com/{{ github_handle }}/platform-infra) stack.

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Backend | FastAPI (Python 3.13), served via DigitalOcean App Platform |
| Database | PostgreSQL 18 on a shared managed cluster (private VPC) |
| Cache / Queue | Valkey 8 on a shared managed instance (Redis-compatible; DO replaced Managed Redis with Valkey in mid-2025) |
{% if include_frontend -%}
| Frontend | Next.js (TypeScript), deployed to DigitalOcean App Platform |
{% endif -%}
{% if include_ios -%}
| iOS | SwiftUI |
{% endif -%}
| Infrastructure | Pulumi (Python), stack `{{ pulumi_org }}/{{ app_name }}/prod` |
| Auth | Clerk |
| Feature Flags | Flagsmith |
| Error Tracking | Sentry |
| Observability | Honeycomb |

## Local Development

### Prerequisites

- Python 3.13+, [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
{% if include_frontend -%}
- Node.js 24+
{% endif -%}
- Docker (for local Postgres + Valkey)

### Backend

```bash
# Copy and fill in environment variables (pydantic-settings reads from backend/.env)
cp backend/.env.example backend/.env

# Start local Postgres + Valkey
docker compose up -d

# Install dependencies and run
cd backend
uv sync
uv run uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

The API will be available at http://localhost:8000.
{% if include_frontend %}

### Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
cp .env.example .env.local
npm install
npm run dev
```

The frontend will be available at http://localhost:3000.
{% endif %}

## Tests

```bash
cd backend
uv run pytest
```

## Environment Variables

See `backend/.env.example`{% if include_frontend %}, `frontend/.env.example`,{% endif %} and the root
`.env.example` reference for required variables and documentation. In production,
these are injected by Pulumi as App Platform secrets — you never manage them manually.

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | PostgreSQL connection string (asyncpg driver) |
| `REDIS_URL` | Redis connection URL |
| `DEBUG` | Enable debug mode (default: `false`) |
| `ENVIRONMENT` | Runtime environment (`development` / `production`) |
{% if include_frontend -%}
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | Backend API URL for the frontend |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | Clerk publishable key for the frontend |
{% endif -%}
| `CLERK_SECRET_KEY` | Clerk authentication secret |
| `FLAGSMITH_API_KEY` | Flagsmith environment API key |
| `SENTRY_DSN` | Sentry DSN for error reporting |
| `HONEYCOMB_API_KEY` | Honeycomb API key for observability |

## Deploying to the shared platform

You need: an `app-deployer` DigitalOcean token and Pulumi access (ask your
platform admin to grant you — see platform-infra → "Granting a new app-owner").

1. Initialise your app stack, set config, and deploy — all from `infra/`:
   ```bash
   cd infra
   pulumi stack init prod           # first time only — you create and own this stack
   # SaaS secrets (values from scripts/setup-saas.sh output):
   pulumi config set --secret clerk_secret_key  <value>
   pulumi config set --secret flagsmith_api_key <value>
   pulumi config set --secret sentry_dsn        <value>
   pulumi config set --secret honeycomb_api_key <value>
{%- if include_frontend %}
   pulumi config set clerk_publishable_key pk_test_<value>   # frontend only, non-secret
{%- endif %}
   pulumi up                        # creates your app + per-app database/user/pool
   ```
   The first deploy may fail to reach the database — that is expected until the
   next step.
2. **Ask your platform admin to onboard this app (one-time):**
   `./scripts/onboard-app.sh {{ app_name }}` (run in platform-infra). This makes
   your app a trusted source and grants your DB user schema privileges.
3. Redeploy (push to `main`, or re-run `pulumi up` from `infra/`).
4. In the Flagsmith dashboard, create the `hello_banner` flag (enabled) for your
   environment so `/feature` reports it on.
5. Get your live URL and verify:
   ```bash
   pulumi stack output app_url                  # https://{{ app_name }}-xxxx.ondigitalocean.app
   curl "$(pulumi stack output app_url)/api/"   # all integrations should report true
   ```

Once onboarded, merging to `main` triggers GitHub Actions (`pulumi up` + App
Platform redeploy) automatically — no manual steps needed for subsequent deploys.

